A||N||8||burdell1||Changing interior-light to dark||||1||
Z||000000||burdell1||02-16-2000||06:56 PM||||Has anyone tried to change the interior of their car to a dark grey from a light grey? Would it be hard to paint it? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||burdell1||02-23-2000||11:03 AM||||How hard would it be to change the color of the headliner to a darker grey?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||02-23-2000||05:02 PM||||I have no idea about the rest of interior, but the headliner is a piece of cake.  Just replace the material with the other color.  Once you get the headliner out (be very careful because it's brittle), it's easy.||||reg||1
Z||000003||burdell1||03-03-2000||12:58 PM||||I just had the headliner replaced around a year or so ago.....How hard would it be to replace the interior with new ones.....Is it hard to get at the screws?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||03-03-2000||01:14 PM||||Recovering/replacing the headliner isn't a big job.  Figure on a few hours though.<P>Remove the trim that outlines the door opening and runs along the windshield.  It is held on by snap clips that are tough if they haven't broken already.  I just broke mine off and replaced them with new ones when I reinstalled the trim.  You can use the universal feather type clips that look like little spruce trees.<P>Remove the upper seat belt mounting bolt.  The plastic cover snaps open to get access to the large torx screw(don't remember the size).<P>Remove the side trim piece that goes from floor to cieling (the one the upper seat belt bolt is in).  It has a split in it to allow for the seat belt to slide out if needed.  (I have an 85SE with headrest speakers.  You may have to remove the speaker grills from the side panel. I'm not familiar with them since I don't have them in my car).<P>Remove the maplight fixture (screws are underneath the clear plastic lenses, they snap out) and unplug the wiring connector.<P>Remove the sunvisors and coat hooks.<P>Remove the trip strip around the sunroof opening and the sunroof latch mechanism (if so equiped).<P>Now carefully slide your arm between the headliner and the roof to pry it loose.  Be careful at the front because I believe there are velcro strips holding it up where the it meets the windshield.  If you accidentally tear these off the headliner backing material you can simply glue them back on (I used the foam backed material spray adhesive that I used to attach the fabric.<P>Now the headliner should drop from the ceiling so you can remove it out the door opening.<P>Before you put the new (or newly recovered) headliner back in clean the black goo off the top edge of the side trim panel so it won't rub black marks on your new headliner when you put them back in (speaking from experience here).||||reg||1
Z||000005||burdell1||03-03-2000||01:18 PM||||How do you replace the interior pieces (the light grey plastic trim)? Is that very hard to do?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Capt Fiero||03-03-2000||01:53 PM||||Now we are in my neck of the woods.  I have changed my interior from red to blue to silver to currently BLACK JET BLACK.  It is actually fun.  First most of the bolts that hold the interior is 7mm with a few 10mm's thrown in for good measure.  If you decide to do it, do it all at once.  You will find yourself taking the same thing off to get 2 diffrent pieces off.  Start with teh door panels. Very Easy.  2 large phililps screws for the arm rest, 2 small philips for the door handel cover, Then go around the edge of the door panel with a large flat tool so that you can pop it off without breaking anything.  Next go to seats.  13mm I believe do the front nuts then get in the car sit down and slide the seat forward get out undo the rear bolts. Repeat for both seats. Unpluge speaker wires if your car has em.  Use a T-50 torx to undo both seatbelt bolts on both sides.  Those bolts are a pain, you may have to tap the torx in with a hammer. Light taps not pounding you will have a nighmare getting the torx out of the srew if you pound it in too hard.  Now move to the center consol.  open glove box behend door is 2 7mm remove both. (you can remove the glove box door string for more access) remove the cig cover 2 small torx remve lighter.  Oh man I could go on for hours on this.  I have removed complete interiors out of Fieros so many times I can recite step by step each bolt, size of the bolt and location of each clip by heart.  It is a about an 8hr job to Remove everything and put it all back in.  Not including painting and prep.  Use a good quality Vinyle paint.  Sims makes a good prep agent.  You want to make sure that there is no armore all on anything.  The dash is a bit of work, but it can be done.  A cordless drill will speed up the removal by 3 fold.  Make sure to get a 1/4" drive adapter for the drill and a socket set that has 6mm-15mm.   It is a fun job you can have the music going get a couple of friends to help.  You can have a real blast.  Oh when you have everything out. If you have access to a steam cleaner, remove the carpets out of the car, clean them and they spray the carpets with whatever color you are using it takes about 2 1/2 cans per side.  You need to spray down until it is almost soaking in paint. Or you will only get the surface of the carpet and it will rub off easy.  Door panels you should remove the door pockets and leave them alone. Seats I do not know what you have so I am going to leave that one alone. But I I would just buy nice seat covers to match what you are doing, no sense in changing the seats.  Hmm.  If you would like some other help with any interior questions or anyone else needs some help with anything call me 604-864-7959.  I think that everyone else can help you, if you are in my area I would gladly come over and give you a hand.  I am in B.C. Canada. Near Vancouver.  <BR>David Just remember pry don't yank. Tap don't pound. But if you do break somthing.  Don't go nuts it can be replaced.||||reg||1
Z||000007||ggarsk||03-03-2000||10:14 PM||||Dupli-Color makes a vinyl paint in charcoal grey that matches the interior gray of the fiero almost perfectly. You can find it at most parts stores or at hardware stores. Try the hardware stores first as it will be cheaper. So far it seems to be wearing good except where my feet hit getting in and out.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Carrolles||03-04-2000||07:30 AM||||I talked to the folks at SEMS about changing color of fabric/carpet.  They say to spray a small area and then bursh it in with a nylon brush.  This works the color down into the fibers without making a hard sticky mess.<P>They have a light grey color that matches my 85SE interior exactly.||||reg||1
